21
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.930
John Talburt: So let's talk a little bit about data categories.

22
00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:55.510
John Talburt: There's many ways to categorize data,

23
00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:07.469
John Talburt: when we try to group it with some common characteristic or feature. And they can be useful, especially when we're talking about data. If you're out there and people are talking about data using certain terms.

24
00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:12.559
John Talburt: It's good to be able to follow them and know what they mean by it.

25
00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:18.110
John Talburt: So here's a big one now with AI, and that's the data categories by structure.

26
00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.779
John Talburt: Structured data, unstructured data, and semi-structured data.

27
00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:28.469
John Talburt: And, what we mean by that is that structured data

28
00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:32.139
John Talburt: Or data that can be directly machine readable.

29
00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:45.180
John Talburt: In other words, that we… our systems are set up so that if we give it structured data, it knows how to read it, it knows how to interpret the data, because it has a formal syntax.

30
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:52.020
John Talburt: just like any natural language, English and so forth, we have a syntax about

31
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:57.619
John Talburt: verbs and nouns and adjectives and so forth. Although our…

32
00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.859
John Talburt: The natural language syntax seems to be very flexible.

33
00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:10.469
John Talburt: Machine-readable syntax, like we would have with a Python program, or XML, or JSON,

34
00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:16.219
John Talburt: It's much more formal, and therefore, it's very predictable, and the computer can read it.

35
00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:24.689
John Talburt: And so, we would have, directly machine-redeemable, for example, relational databases where we have a

36
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:30.399
John Talburt: Schema that, that outlines the, The data and the relationships.

37
00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:38.630
John Talburt: Comma-separated value files, this is what we see a lot, and you're gonna use that in your assignment today.

38
00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:51.160
John Talburt: Although we say comma separated, it just means that the fields are separated by special character. Although it's often a comma, it could be any other type of character, a colon, semicolon.

39
00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.920
John Talburt: Sometimes a pipe character is used,

40
00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:58.270
John Talburt: The, uppercase of,

41
00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:03.680
John Talburt: Where is that? Yeah, the uppercase of the… Backward slash.

42
00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:05.580
John Talburt: On your keyboard.

43
00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:23.249
John Talburt: XML has kind of fallen out of favor these days, but there's still a lot of it around, and it's a markup language that has a formal syntax that uses less than and greater than signs to designate tags and fields.

44
00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.809
John Talburt: And now it's been largely replaced by JSON, which is a somewhat simpler

45
00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:35.779
John Talburt: Markup language that has, a formal syntax to it as well.

46
00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:42.759
John Talburt: So, unstructured data, Our human-readable data.

47
00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:48.649
John Talburt: But not necessarily machine-readable, typically require pre-processing to be machine-readable.

48
00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:53.649
John Talburt: Now, this is changing now, as you're probably well aware.

49
00:04:54.150 --> 00:05:03.220
John Talburt: that, in, in… in… Traditionally, we have to… we… we had to put

50
00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:06.080
John Talburt: In order for a computer to read.

51
00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:10.200
John Talburt: Text, read what we were… was written.

52
00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:14.819
John Talburt: We had to translate it into some kind of structured format.

53
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:26.669
John Talburt: like a comma-separated file, or an XML file, or something, before we could put it into the computer, for the computer to understand, what we were trying to do with the data.

54
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.360
John Talburt: And of course, now that has changed dramatically with large language models.

55
00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:39.260
John Talburt: And so now, most human-readable documents are now In some sense, computer-readable.

56
00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:44.809
John Talburt: In other words, you can put them into these models, and they can read and understand.

57
00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:54.789
John Talburt: And this is term… term… one of the reasons now, or one of the main applications that we're seeing in AI, is to take,

58
00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:05.580
John Talburt: unstructured data, some type of document. I have a student right now working on a project where he's reading an EDGAR document. EDGAR is a database from the,

59
00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:10.439
John Talburt: The, securities and Exchange Commission, where

60
00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:18.109
John Talburt: Corporations have to report, my quarterly and annual reports, or Or reports of significant change.

61
00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:27.789
John Talburt: And he's having the computer or language models read those documents to understand what the document is talking about.

62
00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:34.370
John Talburt: However, we're still not quite to the point that…

63
00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:42.879
John Talburt: We can just feed unstructured documents into the system and do the traditional computing, so we're sort of at a…

64
00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:52.480
John Talburt: A bridge right now, where there's a huge amount of investment in traditional Systems that require structured inputs.

65
00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:56.360
John Talburt: Master Data Management System, invoicing, billing systems.

66
00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:01.000
John Talburt: Customer Relationship Management System, CRM.

67
00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:04.350
John Talburt: All of these systems that are legacy systems.

68
00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:10.110
John Talburt: We're all built to require that the data come in in a certain structured format.

69
00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:14.480
John Talburt: So, what's happening right now, we're in a transition stage.

70
00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:20.839
John Talburt: is that many applications of AI are now reading those unstructured documents.

71
00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:25.099
John Talburt: But the purpose is not to process them and do something with them.

72
00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:36.610
John Talburt: What they're doing is asking the language model to read it, understand it, and then transform it into a structured,

73
00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:40.659
John Talburt: Data record so that it can go into these legacy systems.

74
00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:54.809
John Talburt: We're not quite yet to the point that we are replacing these large legacy systems that have a huge investment in intellectual property. Even huge payroll systems.

75
00:07:54.940 --> 00:08:04.949
John Talburt: SAP-type ERP, enterprise systems, all of these big systems that have huge investments in time and capital.

76
00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:21.729
John Talburt: are not being replaced. Eventually, they probably will be, in what we call semantic computing, where we basically… our systems will operate just like people. We give them documents in any readable format.

77
00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:26.209
John Talburt: The systems can read them, process them, and do all the processing.

78
00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:33.419
John Talburt: The holdback now, to prevent us from doing that right now, Is that,

79
00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:42.499
John Talburt: If you give a large language model, like a document, and ask it to understand what's in that document, and output maybe some kind of structured record.

80
00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.010
John Talburt: It takes time to do that.

81
00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:51.589
John Talburt: As you're probably used to seeing on your screen, when you do that, it'll come up and have a little,

82
00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:55.239
John Talburt: something, a spin, that says, I'm thinking, I'm thinking.

83
00:08:55.410 --> 00:09:07.570
John Talburt: And even though the thinking may be quite fast in human terms, it may only take a few seconds, maybe sometimes 30 seconds or a minute, depending on what you're asking it to do.

84
00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:15.160
John Talburt: This prevents these kinds of systems from being scalable to millions of records, because

85
00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:23.319
John Talburt: These legacy systems that we're using that are based on traditional programming and structured data

86
00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:30.119
John Talburt: are very fast, where they're thousands, hundreds of thousands of times faster

87
00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:44.829
John Talburt: than these models and doing these inferencing, or thinking about doing, reading, for example, a natural language document. So this is kind of where we are right now, and there's a lot of things we're going to look at later in the course.

88
00:09:45.030 --> 00:10:00.949
John Talburt: some of the ways that we're approaching that through what's called Agentic AI, where the model deploys agents, or workers, that can work on these problems, and those tend to be faster.

89
00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:07.480
John Talburt: And another approach that people are taking is what's called embedding.

90
00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:15.140
John Talburt: Where the text is… The model takes the text and translates it into a numeric vector.

91
00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:23.520
John Talburt: A vector that presumably captures The… both the syntactic and the semantic meaning of the text.

92
00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:32.079
John Talburt: Into a numeric vector, and then these numeric vectors can be processed much faster than just simple text.

93
00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:36.469
John Talburt: Anyway, we'll talk more about that as we go, but this is a good time to bring it up.

94
00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:44.560
John Talburt: So, probably need to revise this. So now, unstructured data not only is human-readable, it is machine-readable, to a certain extent.

95
00:10:45.200 --> 00:11:03.809
John Talburt: But again, because of the large investments in traditional systems, most AI is being used to pre-process the… and turn it into something that's structured. So that's a huge application of AI at the moment, is transforming unstructured text into structured text.

96
00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:05.849
John Talburt: Oops, sorry.

97
00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:13.369
John Talburt: And, though it… and, of course, the models can go beyond,

98
00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:20.190
John Talburt: just text. They, they, they, now we have what we call multimodal, models.

99
00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:24.219
John Talburt: That can, read images and audio.

100
00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:31.660
John Talburt: And so, have very human behavior. That's the whole point of AI, is to,

101
00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:34.479
John Talburt: Be able to think and reason as humans.

102
00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:39.460
John Talburt: Semi-structured data is part structured, part unstructured.

103
00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:48.179
John Talburt: But, to some extent, almost all data are semi-structured. It's just a matter of degree.

104
00:11:48.390 --> 00:12:00.410
John Talburt: Take, for example, people would say, well, you know, a relational database is totally structured. It's got a schema, everything is labeled, and we know exactly how things are laid out in it.

105
00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:04.049
John Talburt: But that's really not necessarily true. For example, you look in a…

106
00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:09.920
John Talburt: In a database table, it may have a comments column, which is a free-form text that

107
00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:19.170
John Talburt: you know, is not machine readable, but it would be AI readable. And that, again, that's another application that we see a lot.

108
00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:26.029
John Talburt: In large databases, for example, in telemarketing, where people have called in and left,

109
00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:37.139
John Talburt: a lot of messages or complaints or orders, then now there's a big use of AI to go through all those and read them and understand them and come up with,

110
00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:55.469
John Talburt: with proposals or remedies for, you know, what were the major problems that it found, what things should be addressed. So we're seeing big results now from AI and its ability to read free-form text. And again, same thing, free-form addresses. In a database, you may have an address.

111
00:12:55.660 --> 00:13:00.690
John Talburt: column, but it's… it's the entire street address. The street number, the…

112
00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:04.759
John Talburt: The directional, the street name, the street suffix.

113
00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:11.359
John Talburt: Secondary apartment number, all this kind of thing, all in one, text string.

114
00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:16.330
John Talburt: And again, traditionally, what's had to happen is that we've had to write programs

115
00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:24.910
John Talburt: To go in and, and try to examine that and, and, what we do, what we call parsing.

116
00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:34.280
John Talburt: Where we pull the things apart, and we say, okay, now we're going to pull the street number into a separate column, the street name in a column, and the street suffix, and so forth.

117
00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:37.769
John Talburt: And again, now this is a use of AI.

118
00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:40.400
John Talburt: Is it can read, and

119
00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:45.740
John Talburt: Understand those components and parse them out automatically.

120
00:13:45.860 --> 00:14:03.409
John Talburt: And again… but again, what this is doing is taking unstructured, in this case semi-structured data, and structuring it, because we're still relying primarily on the structured, systems, the systems that require structured input.

121
00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:08.410
John Talburt: But we're beginning to move away from that, and eventually we'll get to the point.

122
00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:11.680
John Talburt: Where the preparation is not necessary.

123
00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:18.159
John Talburt: You can just take the data as it is, put it in your system, payroll system, billing system, whatever it is.

124
00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.839
John Talburt: And, the system won't be able to figure it out.

125
00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:33.770
John Talburt: A good example of a hybrid is like email, right? So you have specifically designated a to address, and a from address, and a date, and a subject.

126
00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:36.130
John Talburt: But the message itself is freeform.

127
00:14:36.590 --> 00:14:47.950
John Talburt: And so traditional systems are not really able to read the message, but again, now with AI and large language models, we're able to read the content of the message and interpret them.

128
00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:54.259
John Talburt: So, another way to categorize data is by function.

129
00:14:56.450 --> 00:15:01.169
John Talburt: You can say, well, there's operational data, that's the data that I'm using to run my business.

130
00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:08.230
John Talburt: And that typically has some subcategories. One of the major ones is master data.

131
00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:12.349
John Talburt: Then there's transactional data and reference data.

132
00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:13.990
John Talburt: and metadata.

133
00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:16.670
John Talburt: So these are some of the primary,

134
00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:24.859
John Talburt: categories of operational data that when you go into any IT system, you'll typically find these.

135
00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:27.330
John Talburt: We'll talk about each one.

136
00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:37.680
John Talburt: Then there's user-generated data that people are typing in, creating, and then machine-generated data from, you know,

137
00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:45.569
John Talburt: I was recently visited with the Alcoa plant, down, in South Arkansas.

138
00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:50.689
John Talburt: And they have these huge machines where they're rolling out aluminum foil.

139
00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:56.259
John Talburt: Matter of fact, almost all the aluminum foil that you see in the grocery store anywhere in the United States.

140
00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:59.769
John Talburt: was probably made down in South Arkansas.

141
00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:03.729
John Talburt: And these machines have monitors that monitor

142
00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:07.510
John Talburt: How fast the rollers are spinning, how much,

143
00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:23.929
John Talburt: leeway, how much tolerance is in there, how much electricity they're using, and all kinds of monitorings and sensors, and that all gets generated. And again, this is another use of AI. Now what's happening is,

144
00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:29.779
John Talburt: These machines, the downtime on those is very costly. They need to run constantly.

145
00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:35.980
John Talburt: And, so one of the things that they're doing is taking all the sensor data that's machine-generated.

146
00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:44.160
John Talburt: Putting it into AI models and using that to predict when a failure might be imminent, so that they can prevent those.

147
00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:52.890
John Talburt: Or slow the machine, stop the machine, and do some kind of adjustment or repair before it actually breaks down for a long period of time.

148
00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:59.699
John Talburt: So, master data is still an important category of data.

149
00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:06.869
John Talburt: And one way to think about master data are nouns of data vocabulary.

150
00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:18.109
John Talburt: So, master data describes people, places, and things, just like we say that's what a noun is in English, right? The word that describes a person, or a place, or a thing.

151
00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:23.079
John Talburt: So, master data are associated with real-world entities.

152
00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:31.140
John Talburt: like a customer, right? There's a real person out there that's a customer that has a unique identity, then you want to track that.

153
00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:39.799
John Talburt: And be sure that when you get two customer transactions, you can tell them apart. Are they the same customer or different customers?

154
00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:56.150
John Talburt: Because each one has a unique identity, and it's very important to your business, right? Customers, your most important thing when you're in a for-profit business, or even government agencies, right? The citizens, people that are using these systems.

155
00:17:56.290 --> 00:17:59.289
John Talburt: Like the Department of Motor Vehicles.

156
00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:10.630
John Talburt: There, they're tracking not only people who are drivers, but also, the real-world things, which are vehicles, right? So they track those and identify those.

157
00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:17.810
John Talburt: And we have nice, unique identifiers, though, we call vehicle identification numbers, or VIN numbers.

158
00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:22.890
John Talburt: And so that's all master data, and that's a characteristic of master data.

159
00:18:23.110 --> 00:18:36.270
John Talburt: Is that we assign a unique identifier to each one of these entities that we're tracking, whether it's a patient, or a vehicle, or a product, and we want… and we use that to identify it.

160
00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:42.280
John Talburt: And the reason we use an identifier rather than, just a description

161
00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:45.760
John Talburt: Is that it's a data quality issue, right?

162
00:18:46.030 --> 00:18:50.530
John Talburt: If we… if we describe a customer only by that customer's name and address.

163
00:18:50.960 --> 00:19:01.479
John Talburt: First of all, those are subject to change. People change their name, they change their addresses, but even if they don't change their names and addresses, they represent them in different ways.

164
00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:06.739
John Talburt: It might be, Bill Talburt, but it could also be Will Talburt.

165
00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:11.039
John Talburt: And… or William Talburt. So,

166
00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:23.199
John Talburt: These… these different ways make it hard to… to know, and so if we're able to give each one a unique identifier, and say this is customer 101,

167
00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:29.870
John Talburt: And we can keep that 101 associated with that customer's information.

168
00:19:30.130 --> 00:19:34.159
John Talburt: Then, it makes life a lot easier in our systems.

169
00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:51.519
John Talburt: If we want to bring together all the customer transactions for a particular customer, it's very easy, if we've assigned all of those transactions, customer identifier 101, we just sort and gather together all the 101 records, and we've got all the sales, or all the buys.

170
00:19:51.690 --> 00:20:06.279
John Talburt: purchases that that customer has made. So it simplifies the processing and ensures that we're not over-counting or under-counting customers or patients, or mixing up their medical charts. We don't want to do that.

171
00:20:06.690 --> 00:20:14.210
John Talburt: So, so master data management is really about what we call semantic encoding.

172
00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:26.099
John Talburt: Semantic encoding is exactly what I've described. If I have a customer, and I say, okay, I want to represent that customer by a unique value, an identifier.

173
00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:35.870
John Talburt: Then I've semantically encoded that record. I've put in that one-on-one value as a stand-in to represent that particular customer.

174
00:20:36.390 --> 00:20:53.229
John Talburt: And so, master data management is all about semantic encoding and, keeping track, keeping those straight, right? For it to work, I have to be sure that every time I see that same customer, I'm giving that… I'm assigning that same identifier.

175
00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:57.990
John Talburt: So anyway, master data are like the nouns of data.

176
00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:01.170
John Talburt: And as I just said, and so again.

177
00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:15.370
John Talburt: We used to have T numbers here, now we have Workday numbers that identify us in our system, right? So, one issue with master data is that, yes, within a particular system or context.

178
00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:25.259
John Talburt: Every, you know, every entity has a unique identifier, but in different systems, in different contexts.

179
00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:34.610
John Talburt: Yes, they'll do the same thing, but they give them different identifiers. So, for example, your workday number here at UALR

180
00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:37.959
John Talburt: If you go and enroll in another college.

181
00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:47.379
John Talburt: Arkansas State? Well, that might not be true. Yeah, it would be true. You go out of state, go to another university in Mississippi.

182
00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:55.339
John Talburt: They'll give you another number. They will want to track you and give you a unique number within their context, but it'll be a different one.

183
00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:57.369
John Talburt: So, it's very difficult

184
00:21:57.620 --> 00:22:03.089
John Talburt: to come up with a universal identifier. As a matter of fact, there's a lot of political

185
00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:08.849
John Talburt: weight against doing that, so… because now this looks like Big Brother.

186
00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:15.939
John Talburt: It's, to some extent, already been done in the United States with social security numbers.

187
00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:29.960
John Talburt: Other countries, where it's not such a big deal for them, especially in Europe and India and other places, people are assigned a unique citizen's number that they have to use for all of their government operations.

188
00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:37.430
John Talburt: Here we say we try to protect the social security number, but we haven't done a very good job of it.

189
00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:40.150
John Talburt: Probably everybody knows what yours is.

190
00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:47.809
John Talburt: The problem with that is that we don't have a universal system for maintaining that alignment that I talked about.

191
00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:54.560
John Talburt: That to be assured that that social security number that's associated with your record is really yours.

192
00:22:54.890 --> 00:22:59.849
John Talburt: So there are a lot of fake social security numbers. We run into problems, for example.

193
00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:06.169
John Talburt: Now, of course, in the U.S, as soon as you're born, you're assigned a social security number.

194
00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:12.850
John Talburt: You used to not get one until you started working around age 16 or 18.

195
00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:16.609
John Talburt: But now you're assigned from the beginning, but even in schools.

196
00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:20.209
John Talburt: Helping schools manage their enrollments.

197
00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:27.309
John Talburt: schools that use social security numbers for their student identifiers, Are often incorrect, because

198
00:23:27.590 --> 00:23:31.020
John Talburt: They enroll a student, and they may not know

199
00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:38.290
John Talburt: what their student's social security number is, so they use their mother's social security number, their father's social security number.

200
00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:43.340
John Talburt: And in many cases where we have foreign workers, sometimes they just make them up.

201
00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:55.270
John Talburt: or steal someone else is Social Security. So, again, we don't have one that's universally unique, at least in the United States. Now, the closest, probably, is a social security number.

202
00:23:57.760 --> 00:24:10.550
John Talburt: So, the problem is that records come in. I mean, if I go to the hospital here and check in, I don't know what my patient number is at St. Vincent's or Baptist Hospital here in town.

203
00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:18.059
John Talburt: So, what has to happen is that somebody has to figure out what's happened, I mean, what my…

204
00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:22.439
John Talburt: identifier is for that particular hospital, Baptist.

205
00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:30.050
John Talburt: So typically, you go to admission desk, and you give them your name and address, and they look you up and search, and they say, okay, this is it.

206
00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:45.199
John Talburt: And that's the reason they put that band around your wrist that has that patient number, so that whenever you go to any particular procedure, you get an x-ray or some dose of medicine, they can scan that barcode on that

207
00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:59.530
John Talburt: on that wristband to be sure that they're appending that… the correct identifier for you as a patient in that hospital to the records that they're generating. Test records, or x-ray records, whatever.

208
00:25:00.220 --> 00:25:10.980
John Talburt: And so, again, the whole point of this is it simplifies the deduplication and integration of records downstream business units, for example, in the hospital, again.

209
00:25:11.050 --> 00:25:21.010
John Talburt: That very easy way to bring together all the treatments that you've had in different departments of the hospital at different times into a single medical chart.

210
00:25:21.950 --> 00:25:28.340
John Talburt: So that's the management master data, it's a very big operation, and there are many vendors in this space.

211
00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:34.340
John Talburt: Now, transactional data is sort of like the verbs, right? There's something happening.

212
00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:44.940
John Talburt: With those entities, right? So, for example, a good example would be, like, a sales order, or a credit card payment, or insurance claim. What's happening is…

213
00:25:46.300 --> 00:26:00.629
John Talburt: you've got a sales associate who's an employee that has an employee number, and the transaction is sold a product which has also is in master data management. So, again, just like in a language, the,

214
00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:09.230
John Talburt: The transactional data sort of represents sentences where you have subjects, verbs and objects.

215
00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:18.639
John Talburt: A sales associate sells a product to a… and again, there would also be a pro… a customer.

216
00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:24.119
John Talburt: So, the sales associate and the product and the customer number are all under Master Data Management.

217
00:26:24.310 --> 00:26:32.769
John Talburt: We want unique identifiers for each one of our customers, unique identifiers for each one of our products, each one of our employees.

218
00:26:32.910 --> 00:26:37.419
John Talburt: And then, in this case, we want a lot of duplication.

219
00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:45.059
John Talburt: In master data management, we don't want duplicates. We don't want to put the same customer in the master data file twice.

220
00:26:45.170 --> 00:26:48.880
John Talburt: Because now it looks like two customers instead of one.

221
00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:56.160
John Talburt: So, we really want to deduplicate our master files that try… we want one-to-one correspondence.

222
00:26:56.270 --> 00:27:02.800
John Talburt: One master record for each entity that we're tracking, if it's a patient, a customer, a product.

223
00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:17.020
John Talburt: On the other hand, transactional data, we want lots of it, right? We would like to sell lots of product P456, right? The more we sell, the more profits that we could make. So there, it's different. We want lots of transactions.

224
00:27:17.170 --> 00:27:21.350
John Talburt: But by embedding, semantically encoding those transactions.

225
00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:27.469
John Talburt: With the identifiers of the master data, then it makes it very easy for us to,

226
00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:41.679
John Talburt: To… to do our downstream systems where we're, we're making invoices, or making sales reports, inventory. We can track those things because we have those unique identifiers.

227
00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:46.819
John Talburt: And then the third type of data is reference data.

228
00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:59.550
John Talburt: And reference data is really very much like master data, except it's simpler in the sense that it really deals with codes, or classification schemes.

229
00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:03.639
John Talburt: That are referenced by, transaction records.

230
00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:07.280
John Talburt: Or a master record, so it… it…

231
00:28:08.060 --> 00:28:15.779
John Talburt: What reference data is, is an agreement on what codes we're going to use so that data becomes interoperable.

232
00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:19.029
John Talburt: Take, for example, here at the university.

233
00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:30.480
John Talburt: Well, you're not here at the university, many of you, but we have codes that represent the buildings on campus, right? So, right now, I'm in the

234
00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:32.419
John Talburt: EIT building.

235
00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:36.830
John Talburt: And the,

236
00:28:36.930 --> 00:28:50.080
John Talburt: It's Electronics, or Engineering… I'm sorry, Engineering and Information Technology Building. It's abbreviated as EIT. But we want to use that, we want to consistently use that code so that there's no confusion

237
00:28:50.230 --> 00:29:09.880
John Talburt: If somebody else is using a different code, then again, it's the same problem as master data management. But in the idea of reference data is that we don't have to semantically encode it. In other words, I don't want to go to another step and another abstraction and say, well, here's the engineering and information technology building.

238
00:29:10.140 --> 00:29:13.760
John Talburt: I don't want to assign… try to assign it a…

239
00:29:13.990 --> 00:29:24.539
John Talburt: a numeric identifier to track it, because it's very simple. If I just consistently use EIT, it's a single value.

240
00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:28.169
John Talburt: The same thing with postal codes, right?

241
00:29:28.330 --> 00:29:33.140
John Talburt: So, Arkansas is… standard postal code is capital A, capital R.

242
00:29:33.460 --> 00:29:47.960
John Talburt: And so, as long as we consistently use that in an address context, it's clear that if I see AR, it means Arkansas, and if I want to use Arkansas, I should encode it as AR.

243
00:29:48.380 --> 00:29:49.609
John Talburt: So,

244
00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:56.569
John Talburt: So it's somewhat similar to master data, but it's a little simpler in that we're really dealing mainly with codes.

245
00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:08.770
John Talburt: And by using the same codes, for example, in courses here on campus, everybody understands that when they see the code EIT by a class listing or course listing.

246
00:30:08.930 --> 00:30:12.959
John Talburt: They know that it's referring to a particular building on campus.

247
00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:24.490
John Talburt: And so, reference data is very helpful, very key to data integration and data interoperability, just like, master data.

248
00:30:25.760 --> 00:30:34.479
John Talburt: So, we see a lot of those. Postal codes, universal product codes, things like that. So, some of the codes may be from outside.

249
00:30:34.690 --> 00:30:47.469
John Talburt: That are kind of standard. For example, ISO has many, many standard codes for, geographic, like the codes that we were talking about for states, but also for countries, for…

250
00:30:47.600 --> 00:30:55.500
John Talburt: For, the money, currency, codes,

251
00:30:55.850 --> 00:31:05.439
John Talburt: And so forth. So there are many sort of agreed on nationally or international for postal codes would be within the United States, or agreed upon.

252
00:31:05.610 --> 00:31:08.560
John Talburt: ISO codes, as would be,

253
00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:13.099
John Talburt: you know, universally agreed upon by all countries that participate in ISO.

254
00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:19.469
John Talburt: So those are external, but again, we often have internal code, just like I was talking about.

255
00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:26.430
John Talburt: Unfortunately, ISO doesn't have a set of codes for the buildings on the UA Little Rock campus.

256
00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:36.600
John Talburt: We build those and maintain those on our own, as well as our job codes here, or so forth and so on. So, the reference data…

257
00:31:36.620 --> 00:31:47.420
John Talburt: Could be that we require it to be some kind of national or international standard, or we invent our own for our particular context.

258
00:31:48.630 --> 00:31:52.440
John Talburt: So, metadata, the fourth type of data.

259
00:31:54.060 --> 00:32:05.770
John Talburt: Metadata literally means data about data. So, again, if you have raw data values, then, You…

260
00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:10.890
John Talburt: they can be very ambiguous, right? I have a date,

261
00:32:11.260 --> 00:32:13.359
John Talburt: that I have a date value.

262
00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:23.679
John Talburt: If I just give you a date value, then you know intrinsically what it means, and if you recognize the format, and you say, well, oh.

263
00:32:23.860 --> 00:32:26.409
John Talburt: This is, July the 4th,

264
00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:30.549
John Talburt: 1959. I recognize that by the layout of the

265
00:32:30.820 --> 00:32:46.419
John Talburt: But what is the meaning? What is the semantics of that? I mean, it means a particular date in the calendar, but in a particular application, what does it mean? Is it a date of birth? Is it a date of a sales transaction? It could be many, many things.

266
00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:49.380
John Talburt: So metadata helps to clear that up.

267
00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:53.959
John Talburt: if I have a field of dates, and I label it as,

268
00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:59.040
John Talburt: dates of birth, and I know that what I'm describing with those dates are

269
00:32:59.190 --> 00:33:02.890
John Talburt: The dates of birth were the associated entity.

270
00:33:03.610 --> 00:33:10.630
John Talburt: And so they label, describe, and characterize other data to make it easier to retrieve, interpret, or use.

271
00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:20.589
John Talburt: Technical metadata is data that… and business metadata are two major types of metadata.

272
00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:28.609
John Talburt: Technical metadata describes technology systems, data structures, data quality rules, data standards.

273
00:33:29.050 --> 00:33:34.239
John Talburt: Business data is more about how the operations are to be performed. What are the rules?

274
00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:43.249
John Talburt: You know, can… can a product be returned? Does it require receipt? Is it… can it be returned after 30 days?

275
00:33:43.310 --> 00:33:56.610
John Talburt: different types of rules about the conduct of the business, or… again, I keep saying business, but this could be any organization. It could be, like, a hospital, or it could be, like, a government agency.

276
00:33:57.110 --> 00:34:03.769
John Talburt: So those are the two different types of, metadata that we typically encounter.

277
00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:08.659
John Talburt: And there are many metadata management, tools.

278
00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:17.720
John Talburt: one that's become very popular now with the, and, and metadata…

279
00:34:18.739 --> 00:34:22.580
John Talburt: It has become very important in the area of data governance.

280
00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:25.969
John Talburt: So, as we mentioned earlier in this lecture.

281
00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:30.530
John Talburt: Data governance are the rules for making decisions about data.

282
00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:32.839
John Talburt: What rules do you have to follow?

283
00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:40.600
John Talburt: And so, in some case… in some ways, data governance is about managing metadata.

284
00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:48.670
John Talburt: the… So, data catalogs are intended to describe the data that you have in an organization.

285
00:34:49.330 --> 00:35:01.580
John Talburt: track what's in it, the description of what's in the data, where it's located, what are its characteristics. So data catalogs have become very important, and they're huge tools that vendors have.

286
00:35:01.750 --> 00:35:10.049
John Talburt: To help in tracking data, because if you don't know the metadata, the data are pretty much useless to you, right?

287
00:35:10.240 --> 00:35:13.259
John Talburt: And we have… I wrote an article.

288
00:35:13.550 --> 00:35:17.439
John Talburt: A few years ago, called Data Littering.

289
00:35:18.170 --> 00:35:25.010
John Talburt: And it was at a time when, there was a lot of emphasis on data literacy, so people thought I just misspoke.

290
00:35:25.140 --> 00:35:31.029
John Talburt: And said, I meant to say data literacy, but I said data literary. But no, I meant it,

291
00:35:31.180 --> 00:35:38.069
John Talburt: explicitly. And what I was talking about is data littering is distributing data without metadata.

292
00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:47.860
John Talburt: You have a file, you're working with it, you and a buddy know what's going on, you're exchanging the file back and forth, a spreadsheet or something.

293
00:35:47.970 --> 00:35:53.690
John Talburt: But it really doesn't have adequate description of what's in the data, when it was captured, what it was for.

294
00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:59.139
John Talburt: And that gets out there somewhere, somebody finds it, or it gets on a…

295
00:35:59.260 --> 00:36:07.090
John Talburt: a thumb drive or something, and you're basically littering data around, because you kind of… it's like throwing a…

296
00:36:07.280 --> 00:36:09.940
John Talburt: Wrapper out of a car onto the street.

297
00:36:10.090 --> 00:36:11.690
John Talburt: You know, it's…

298
00:36:11.930 --> 00:36:23.979
John Talburt: you've thrown it out there, no, and what's the… it's not useful, right? It's not useful anymore because it doesn't have that metadata associated with it, so I call that data littering.

299
00:36:26.390 --> 00:36:36.869
John Talburt: Business glossary, so data catalogs tend to be like data dictionaries that really describe the content of a particular file or a particular database.

300
00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:44.110
John Talburt: Business glossaries, Are more on the side of understanding how that data

301
00:36:44.330 --> 00:36:51.209
John Talburt: So some data is sort of native data. In other words, if you Take, payroll data, right?

302
00:36:51.330 --> 00:37:03.429
John Talburt: So, the native data in a payroll system would be the data about, you know, who's the employee, how many hours did they work, or are they on a salary, are they hourly? The information describing that.

303
00:37:03.790 --> 00:37:12.110
John Talburt: But then, you're… those items of data are sort of given items, native items, that come out of,

304
00:37:12.550 --> 00:37:14.190
John Talburt: The system.

305
00:37:14.640 --> 00:37:19.239
John Talburt: And so… but you're going to generate data off of that data.

306
00:37:19.430 --> 00:37:32.669
John Talburt: You're going to take those hours, you're going to take the rate of pay, and so forth, and you're going to compute a new data value, which is the gross pay, the net pay, the deductions. There'll be a whole other set of items

307
00:37:32.810 --> 00:37:40.510
John Talburt: That did not come into the company through some file, their data that's generated to run the business.

308
00:37:40.910 --> 00:38:00.229
John Talburt: And so, the business glossary should describe those items that are not necessarily out there in a file, but are generated in the operation of the company, and exactly how those are generated, what was the logic in generating, what was the logic, how was your net pay calculated?

309
00:38:00.400 --> 00:38:14.509
John Talburt: When I say this item of value, or this data value is your net pay, you need something that describes beyond just saying this is what's going to be on your paycheck. You need something that says, how did I calculate that?

310
00:38:17.510 --> 00:38:23.610
John Talburt: So there's processes as well that, in metadata documents that run,

311
00:38:24.170 --> 00:38:29.720
John Talburt: Systems, the… how those systems are to be run, what are the rules for running those.

312
00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:37.949
John Talburt: And business process management tools that automate. And this is another place where AI is starting to come into,

313
00:38:38.450 --> 00:38:45.490
John Talburt: the picture, because now what we're looking at is what we call Agentic AI.

314
00:38:45.850 --> 00:38:53.560
John Talburt: And in the early days… early days, it's only been a couple years ago, of large language models.

315
00:38:53.920 --> 00:39:13.099
John Talburt: You know, I mean, it's very fascinating. We… but they were more prompt in response, right? It's like having a dialogue. We call them chatbots, right? So we… we type something in, some sentence or some question, could be long, or give it a document, and we say… and we ask it a question about, you know, what…

316
00:39:13.360 --> 00:39:17.509
John Talburt: what should I do here if I've got these symptoms?

317
00:39:17.920 --> 00:39:24.329
John Talburt: And it would think about it, and it would give a response. So it's this idea of prompt and response, prompt and response.

318
00:39:24.690 --> 00:39:27.290
John Talburt: But now, there's an… another,

319
00:39:27.590 --> 00:39:31.340
John Talburt: Facet that's been developed, and that is this idea of

320
00:39:31.690 --> 00:39:40.120
John Talburt: The large language model being able to do something, rather than just respond in text.

321
00:39:40.400 --> 00:39:53.460
John Talburt: you can actually ask it to do something, and it can operate other systems. It can go in and read something from a database, or it can write an email, or it can write a ticket that goes in your JIRA system.

322
00:39:53.780 --> 00:39:57.929
John Talburt: So, it can be connected with other systems to do things.

323
00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:00.969
John Talburt: And not only that, it can be,

324
00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:09.420
John Talburt: It can make decisions, it can evaluate, and basically become like an employee in the company.

325
00:40:09.610 --> 00:40:14.770
John Talburt: To doing things, and so now this has opened up a whole new world of Agentic AI.

326
00:40:15.020 --> 00:40:18.780
John Talburt: Where people are creating these little,

327
00:40:19.190 --> 00:40:24.879
John Talburt: computer employees that work inside the computer, and they can do things. They can…

328
00:40:25.100 --> 00:40:37.499
John Talburt: book, an airline ticket for you, and they can evaluate, make decisions. They can say, no, there's no seats available on this, I need to go back. What's another way to get to Chicago?

329
00:40:37.590 --> 00:40:45.509
John Talburt: Is there another flight? All the things that you would do if you were sitting at a console trying to book tickets for your airline.

330
00:40:45.720 --> 00:40:59.090
John Talburt: You can get agents, just like a real person travel agent, you can have a computer agent and a large language model that can go through and make those kind of decisions and loop back on itself.

331
00:40:59.370 --> 00:41:01.880
John Talburt: So this has opened up quite a bit of,

332
00:41:02.350 --> 00:41:09.450
John Talburt: versatility. Again, the only big issue that we're running into there is… well, there's a couple of them. One is.

333
00:41:09.620 --> 00:41:18.010
John Talburt: Again, the large language models, even though they can reason, are probabilistic models, and sometimes,

334
00:41:18.310 --> 00:41:27.049
John Talburt: they, what we call, hallucinate, which is really nothing more than making a bad prediction. Language models are predictive machines that

335
00:41:27.450 --> 00:41:34.960
John Talburt: you give it words, and they try to predict what the next word is going to be. So they're very probabilistic in their operation.

336
